 ],
 "note": "args.canvas makes the picture, w x h x 3: bytes first (with only \"flat\", that colour everywhere; with \"mul\" and \"add\", pixel i, counted row by row from the top left, has the channels (mul[c] * i + add[c]) % 256), then, in float64, byte * gain + bias (when \"gain\" is there; both float.hex()), then the cast to \"dtype\" (float32 or float64). The result: expect.returned is \"img\" when Python returned the very array it was given (in place, or untouched) and \"new\" when it made one; expect.dtype is the dtype of what came back. The picture is stored as its difference from a baseline: expect.baseline is \"canvas\" (the picture as made) or \"bytes\" (np.clip(canvas, 0, 255).astype(np.uint8), which is what a drawing call that draws starts from; one that draws nothing returns the picture itself, and its baseline is \"canvas\"); expect.box [x0, y0, x1, y1] bounds the pixels that differ from the baseline (null: none do) and \"out\" is that part of the result, stored as uint8 when every value in it is a whole number from 0 to 255 (expect.stored says which dtype). Outside the box the result equals the baseline. apply_dim(img, t, keep): keep is a list of [x, y, r]; x and y are numpy float64 scalars, as kit.picture passes them (cam.wp), so the lit discs are computed in float64. The result is float64 and differs from the picture nearly everywhere, so it is stored thinned: expect.step n means \"out\" holds the pixels i = 0, n, 2 n, ... (counted row by row), (k, 3); expect.box is then absent. speed_lines(img, t, key, x, y, r, direction, n, length, seed): x, y and r as numpy float64.",
